  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 349 PM EDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nCurrently, satellite imagery and ground observations show a solid \nsheet of high clouds over all of Indiana and Kentucky with some mid-\nlevel clouds around 10k feet. At the surface, winds are fairly light \nand variable out of a general southeasterly direction. Most areas \nwill see temperatures reach into the 70s. Some areas in southeastern \nIndiana and the eastern Bluegrass may only see the upper 60s.\n\nTonight, a shortwave riding along the axis of the upper trough will \nlift from near the Ozarks eastward into the Lower Ohio Valley. \nSurface high pressure, centered to our east, keeps a flow of \nmoisture and southeasterly winds over the CWA. A weak low level jet \nwill form just to our west and try to ease into central Kentucky and \nsouthern Indiana, but it's expected to remain fairly weak, around 20-\n35 knots in pockets. This will begin to produce showers with chances \nincreasing through the night. There is a decent amount of dry air \nunder 700 mb that any rain will have to overcome before reaching the \nground. This is expected to limit totals before rain ends Sunday \nnight. The best chances for rain remain near Bowling Green and in \nareas farther to the southwest. The forecast currently has totals \naround 0.5\" in that area tapering to the north and east to just a \nhundredth or two near Madison, Indiana and areas near Richmond, KY \nand east.  \n\nBehind the shortwave, the next system with a couple of embedded \nsurface lows in a large upper trough will bring a north to south \noriented cold front towards the Lower Ohio Valley. Ahead of the \nsystem, an increasing pressure gradient pushing on the outgoing \nsurface high will result in southerly winds reaching 10-15 mph on \nMonday, but this will increase to 20-25 mph by Tuesday lifting highs \ninto the mid to upper 70s across the CWA Tuesday afternoon before \nshowers ahead of the front work east into the CWA Tuesday night. This \nwill be our better chance for rain as widespread rain and showers \nare expected be east of the CWA Wednesday night. Another upper low/ \nshowerwave could bring a quick moving shot of additional showers \nThursday night into Friday morning. In total the area is looking to \nreceive between 1.25 and 3.5 inches with the current forecast.",
